+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Shipped              |
+
+Every tool declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` in its `ToolDefinition` (`src/types/ai.ts`), and that schema is handed verbatim to the model providers so the model knows what a call should look like. Until now nothing checked the call the model actually produced against it: `ToolRegistry.execute` (`src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts`) looked a tool up and handed its executor whatever arrived, so each executor validated its own input — differently, or not at all. The shipped weather tool is the careful case, casting and defaulting `location` and still answering a malformed call with a forecast for `'Unknown'`. This spec states the contract the registry enforces instead, so a malformed call fails in one predictable, known way before any tool code runs.
+
+## The contract
+
+- `ToolRegistry.execute` validates the tool call's `input` against the resolved tool definition's `inputSchema` before the executor runs
+  ([validated by: rejects a missing required property before the executor runs and names it as required](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L41)).
+- A call the schema rejects is answered with a returned `ToolResponse`, not a throw: its `result` describes the failure and names the missing property as required
+  ([validated by: rejects a missing required property before the executor runs and names it as required](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L41)).
+- A rejected wrong-typed field is named by its property path and the expected type, and a rejection names no property value
+  ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed field naming the path and expected type, never the value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L70)).
+- A value outside a declared `enum` is rejected, and the rejection names the property
+  ([validated by: rejects a value outside a declared enum, naming the property](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L85)).
+- Valid input reaches the executor unchanged and exactly once, properties the schema does not list included: validation neither injects `additionalProperties: false`, nor coerces types, nor applies defaults
+  ([validated by: hands valid input to the executor unchanged, extras included, exactly once](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L55)).
+- `register` compiles a static tool's schema at registration time and refuses a schema that cannot compile, naming the tool, so a broken schema fails at startup rather than mid-conversation
+  ([validated by: refuses an uncompilable schema at registration, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L103)).
+- A schema produced by a function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved and enforced per call, against the schema current at that call rather than a stale cached one
+  ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at that call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L121)).
+- A static tool's schema is compiled once and the compiled schema reused across calls
+  ([validated by: compiles a static schema once across ten execute calls](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L138)).
+- Nothing a rejected payload carries — its property values above all — appears in the returned rejection or in a log line, because tool input is where identifying data enters the engine and the engine redacts nothing
+  ([validated by: keeps rejected input values out of the rejection and the log](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L176)).
+- `execute` still throws `Unknown tool: <name>` for an unregistered tool name; that failure stays an exception on purpose, unlike a schema rejection
+  ([validated by: still throws Unknown tool for an unregistered name](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L165)).
+- A rejected tool call is handed back to the model as an ordinary tool result within the existing tool-round budget, so `processMessage` resolves rather than rejects and a corrected retry succeeds
+  ([validated by: returns a rejected tool call to the model as a tool result the next round can correct](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts#L16)).
+
+## Why the rejection is returned rather than thrown
+
+A throw in `execute` propagates through the orchestrator's tool-call `Promise.all`, out of the processing loop's `try`, fires `onError`, and reaches the WebSocket error handler, which sends a generic failure and never sends the stream-end frame. The conversation is over because one tool call was malformed. Returned as a `ToolResponse`, the rejection is exactly what a tool result already is: something the model reads on its next round and corrects, bounded by the same tool-round budget that bounds every other loop. That is also why the `Unknown tool:` throw stays a throw despite being the same failure class the opposite way — changing it affects direct callers, and it is tracked separately.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Validating tool _output_ against anything; the schema governs what enters an executor, not what leaves it.
+- Hallucination detection, an evaluation harness, retry, timeout and rate limiting around tool execution.
+- Per-tool authorization: a schema-valid call for someone else's data is still valid. Authorization is a `ToolContext` boundary concern owned by no issue in this epic; the `beforeToolCall` veto seam is not an authorization model.
+- Any concrete tool implementation, which is written against this contract later.
+
+## File reference
+
+| File                                                    | What it holds                                                                                    |
+| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| `src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts`                   | `ToolRegistry.execute`, where validation runs, and `register`, where a static schema is compiled |
+| `src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts`   | The acceptance tests for every statement above but the last                                      |
+| `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts` | The tool-loop acceptance test for the rejection-to-retry round trip                              |
